---
import CaseStudy from "@/layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import StarterDemo from "@/components/StarterDemo.astro";
import { projects } from "@/data/projects";

const project = projects.find((p) => p.slug === "blockwork-one")!;
---

<CaseStudy project={project}>
  <section class="case-block" aria-labelledby="try-title">
    <h2 id="try-title" data-reveal>Try it</h2>
    <div data-reveal>
      <p class="case-lede" style="margin-bottom: 28px;">
        The app starts with a starter: marks, set, a random hold, a beep. Here's the browser version — sound on.
      </p>
      <StarterDemo />
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="prose-case" data-reveal>
    <h2>The problem with training alone</h2>
    <p>
      A block start lasts well under a second, and almost everything that matters in it is invisible from inside it:
      where your hips sit in the set position, whether the front leg finishes its push, whether your first step lands
      underneath you or in front. A coach sees it. A sprinter on their own sees nothing — and has nobody to call the
      start in the first place.
    </p>

    <h2>Hands-free, by design</h2>
    <p>
      You can't hold a phone and get into blocks, so the whole loop runs without a touch. Prop it on its side, tap
      Start, walk to your mark. It calls you up, waits until you've settled, calls set, holds a random interval, and
      fires. By the time you've walked back, it has found the beep in the recording, tracked your body through the
      start, scored the rep, and said out loud what to fix next.
    </p>

    <h2>Honest numbers</h2>
    <p>
      Every measure has a stated target range and a reason, and anything the camera can't measure reliably — like
      reaction time, which reads later than a block sensor — is tracked rather than graded. When it's too dark to find
      you, it says so and switches to a timed start instead of inventing a result.
    </p>
  </div>
</CaseStudy>
